## Proof

**Setup.** $S$ is the $\ell_1$-ball (cross-polytope) in $\mathbb{R}^{2012}$, with vertices $\{\pm e_j : 1 \le j \le 2012\}$. $T$ is the $\ell_\infty$-sphere of radius $2$, i.e., the boundary of the hypercube $[-2,2]^{2012}$.

**Metric projection onto $S$.** Since $S$ is closed and convex, the closest point to $p$ in $S$ is the unique metric projection $\text{proj}_S(p)$. For the $\ell_1$-ball, this projection is given by soft-thresholding: if $\|p\|_1 \le 1$, the projection is $p$ itself; otherwise, there is a unique threshold $\lambda > 0$ with
$$\sum_{i=1}^{2012} \max(|p_i| - \lambda,\, 0) = 1,$$
and the projection is $q$ with $q_i = \operatorname{sgn}(p_i)\max(|p_i|-\lambda, 0)$.

**When is the projection a vertex?** The projection $q = \pm e_j$ is a vertex iff exactly one coordinate $q_j = \pm 1$ is nonzero and all others are $0$. This requires:

1. A unique index $j$ with $|p_j| > \lambda$ and $|p_j| - \lambda = 1$ (so $|p_j| = 1+\lambda$).
2. All other $|p_i| \le \lambda$ (so $q_i = 0$).

**The surviving coordinate must be the maximum.** Since $p \in T$, we have $\max_i |p_i| = 2$. The surviving coordinate satisfies $|p_j| = 1 + \lambda \le 2$, giving $\lambda \le 1$. If the maximum $2$ were attained at some $k \ne j$, then $|p_k| = 2 > 1 \ge \lambda$, so $k$ would also survive — contradicting uniqueness. Hence $|p_j| = 2$ (the max is at $j$) and $\lambda = 1$.

Conversely, if $|p_j| = 2$ is the **unique** maximum and all $|p_i| \le 1$ for $i \ne j$, then with $\lambda = 1$: $\sum_i \max(|p_i|-1,0) = (2-1) + 0 = 1$ ✓, and the projection is $\pm e_j$. So:

> **The projection is a vertex $\iff$ exactly one coordinate has $|p_i| = 2$ and all others satisfy $|p_i| \le 1$.**

(The boundary cases where another coordinate equals exactly $\pm 1$ or $\pm 2$ have measure zero on $T$ and do not affect the probability.)

**Computing the probability.** The uniform distribution on $T$ (boundary of $[-2,2]^{2012}$) assigns equal weight to each of the $2 \times 2012$ faces by symmetry. On the face $x_j = 2$, the remaining $2011$ coordinates are i.i.d. uniform on $[-2, 2]$.

On this face, $x_j = 2$ is automatically the unique maximum (others are $< 2$ a.s.). The vertex condition reduces to $|x_i| \le 1$ for all $i \ne j$. Each coordinate is uniform on $[-2,2]$ (length $4$), and $|x_i| \le 1$ has length $2$, so
$$\Pr\bigl(|x_i| \le 1\bigr) = \frac{2}{4} = \frac{1}{2}.$$
By independence over the $2011$ free coordinates:
$$\Pr(\text{vertex} \mid \text{face } x_j=2) = \left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^{2011}.$$

By symmetry this is identical on every face, so the overall probability is:

$$\boxed{\dfrac{1}{2^{2011}}}.$$

